options:
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    author: W5TSU
    catch_exceptions: 'True'
    comment: 'Receives Thetis''s real over-the-air TX output (RADE V1 or FreeDV 700E,
      whichever the operator has armed via ZZDK/ZZEF and keyed) via HackRF, demodulates
      it as a standard USB/SSB signal (complex band-pass filter selecting the positive-frequency
      voice passband, then real part -- the standard software-defined way to do SSB
      demod when you have full I/Q, no phasing-method analog circuitry needed), and
      records the result to a mono wav. That wav is the input for an independent decode
      check: freedv-gui''s own reference decoder (''freedv -ut rx -utmode 700E'' or
      ''-utmode RADEV1'', see a local freedv-gui checkout) or Tools/FreeDV/make_fdv_test_iq.py''s
      own analysis conventions -- this flowgraph only captures and demodulates, it
      does not attempt to decode FreeDV/RADE itself. This is the missing half of the
      existing tx_700e_hackrf.grc / tx_radev1_hackrf.grc pair (which transmit a known-good
      signal *into* Thetis''s RX): this one listens to what Thetis''s TX chain actually
      puts out, closing the ''no off-air decode confirmation'' gap noted throughout
      FreeDV-Plan.md for both RADE V1 TX and FreeDV 700E TX.'
    copyright: ''
    description: Off-air capture of Thetis's own RADE V1 / FreeDV 700E TX output,
      via HackRF RX, for independent reference-decoder confirmation
    gen_linking: dynamic
    generate_options: qt_gui
    generator_class_name: PythonQtGuiGenerator
    generator_module: gnuradio.grc.workflows.python_qt_gui
    hier_block_src_path: '.:'
    id: rx_offair_capture_hackrf
    max_nouts: '0'
    output_language: python
    qt_qss_theme: ''
    realtime_scheduling: ''
    run: 'True'
    run_command: '{python} -u {filename}'
    thread_safe_setters: ''
    title: Thetis TX Off-Air Capture -- HackRF RX
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [10, 10]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled

blocks:
- name: audio_rate
  id: variable
  parameters:
    comment: Output wav rate -- matches freedv_get_modem_sample_rate() for both 700E
      and RADE V1 (8 kHz) exactly, so the captured audio can be fed straight to freedv-gui's
      reference decoder with no rate ambiguity (unlike Tools/FreeDV/make_fdv_test_iq.py's
      --input-wav, which does handle arbitrary rates -- this file is for the reference-decoder
      path instead, which is pickier).
    value: '8000'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [24, 176.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: rx_bb_gain
  id: variable_qtgui_range
  parameters:
    comment: HackRF baseband gain -- same start-low guidance as rx_if_gain above.
    gui_hint: 0,2,1,1
    label: RX BB Gain (dB)
    min_len: '200'
    orient: QtCore.Qt.Horizontal
    rangeType: int
    start: '0'
    step: '1'
    stop: '40'
    value: '20'
    widget: counter_slider
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [600, 16.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: rx_if_gain
  id: variable_qtgui_range
  parameters:
    comment: HackRF IF (VGA) gain. Thetis's real TX is a strong, close signal compared
      to typical off-air DX, so this doesn't need the TX flowgraphs' full range --
      but an initial live test found 8dB (this flowgraph's original default) too conservative
      for FreeDV 700E's signal specifically (RADE V1 synced fine at 8dB, 700E didn't
      sync at all until raised to 24dB) -- 20dB here is a revised starting point from
      that finding, matching the TX flowgraphs' own default. Watch for a flat-topped/clipped
      time-domain trace as a sign this or rx_bb_gain is too high, which would corrupt
      the very thing being verified.
    gui_hint: 0,1,1,1
    label: RX IF/VGA Gain (dB)
    min_len: '200'
    orient: QtCore.Qt.Horizontal
    rangeType: int
    start: '0'
    step: '1'
    stop: '40'
    value: '20'
    widget: counter_slider
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [400, 12]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: samp_rate
  id: variable
  parameters:
    comment: HackRF RX front-end rate. 2 Msps -- comfortably above the ~2 Msps floor
      the TX flowgraphs found HackRF needs for reliable quality (same hardware, same
      floor applies to RX), and a clean multiple of audio_rate (250:1) so the frequency-xlating
      filter's decimation is an exact integer.
    value: '2000000'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [120, 176.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: blocks_complex_to_real_0
  id: blocks_complex_to_real
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: Taking the real part after a one-sided (positive-frequency-only) complex
      band-pass IS how software SSB/USB demod works with full I/Q -- no separate phasing-method
      mixer stage needed.
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    vlen: '1'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [696, 536.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: blocks_wavfile_sink_0
  id: blocks_wavfile_sink
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    append: 'False'
    bits_per_sample1: FORMAT_PCM_16
    bits_per_sample2: FORMAT_PCM_16
    bits_per_sample3: FORMAT_VORBIS
    bits_per_sample4: FORMAT_PCM_16
    comment: Runs until you press GRC's stop button (unlike the TX flowgraphs, which
      play a fixed-length wav once and stop automatically -- there's no natural end-of-signal
      marker on receive, so start this flowgraph, key Thetis's TX per the usual thetisctl
      --confirm-tx protocol, let the transmission run, then stop this once Thetis
      unkeys).
    file: out_file
    format: FORMAT_WAV
    nchan: '1'
    samp_rate: audio_rate
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [920, 496.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: center_freq
  id: parameter
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: Must match whatever Thetis is actually transmitting on -- 14.236 MHz
      / DIGU is this project's established calling frequency for both RADE V1 and
      700E testing (see Tools/thetis-ai-control's freedv-calling-frequencies table),
      same default the TX flowgraphs use. Change if Thetis is tuned elsewhere, but
      keep this and Thetis's dial frequency identical -- there is no frequency search
      here, just a fixed-tuned USB demod.
    hide: none
    label: Center Frequency (Hz)
    short_id: ''
    type: eng_float
    value: '14236000'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [180, 12]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: freq_xlating_fir_filter_xxx_0
  id: freq_xlating_fir_filter_xxx
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    center_freq: '0'
    comment: 'USB demod + decimate to audio_rate in one block: a complex band-pass
      covering only +300..+2700 Hz (the positive-frequency side -- USB) rejects the
      mirror-image LSB content that a real-valued filter couldn''t distinguish. center_freq=0
      since osmosdr_source is already tuned exactly to center_freq (the dial frequency),
      so no further shift is needed here, only filtering + decimation.'
    decim: samp_rate // audio_rate
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    samp_rate: samp_rate
    taps: firdes.complex_band_pass(1, samp_rate, 300, 2700, 300)
    type: ccc
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [440, 504.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
